<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">On April 6, 2024, 16-year-old Harper Tillman borrowed his friend’s e-scooter to take a quick trip down the street. Tillman was forced to ride on the street due to foot traffic on the sidewalk. The teenager ran a stop sign and was struck by a car. Soon after his body hit the pavement, he began to see flashing lights. He was paralyzed by confusion and pain. The crash left him with a concussion and a fractured spine, which forced him into a neck brace for months. His story is one of many cautionary tales about the safety issues presented by electric scooters, also known as e-scooters.</span></p>
<p> </p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">E-scooters are intended to be a sustainable form of transportation that is quick and easy to use, but they are also unpredictable. Riders tend to avoid using protective gear, such as helmets and body pads. Minimal policies are put into place to protect riders and pedestrians on the streets. A 2023 study published by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) found that over 70% of riders who faced injuries were not wearing helmets at the time of their crash, increasing the severity of head injuries and fractures. E-scooter riders are twice as likely to suffer from head injuries than bicyclists due to lower rates of wearing helmets, which only 15% of e-scooter riders do.</span></p>
<p> </p>
<p><img class="post__image" src="file:///Users/nicoleclement/Documents/Publii/sites/smpa-3230/input/media/posts/6/Bike-vs-EScooter-Injuries.png" alt="An outline of the human body shows that bike riders have worse injuries in the torso, lower limbs, and road rash/abrasions. Internal injuries, upper limb fractures, and the head are more common in e-scooter riders." width="656" height="874"></p>
<div> </div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In the District, hospital admissions for e-scooter injuries increased by 18% in 2024, according to the District Department of Transportation (DDOT). Tillman was lucky to have faced injuries that healed in just months.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">In Washington, DC, 52 people died in 2024 from e-scooter accidents, which is the same amount of e-scooter deaths in 2023. Between 2022-24, 433 severe injuries and over 3,600 minor injuries affected pedestrians, bicyclists, and e-scooter users due to the unsafe conditions for e-scooter riders. Most of the 68 fatal accidents occurred when motor vehicle drivers hit a person riding a bicycle or e-scooter, according to DDOT.</span><noscript><img src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/b5Bxp/full.png" alt="" /></noscript></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">E-scooter collisions among other riders and bicyclists are rare. In three years, DDOT recorded seven crashes between riders colliding with each other. In other situations, riders tend to lose control of their e-scooters, causing a lot of pedestrian collisions on the sidewalks. Three of five e-scooter injuries occurred on sidewalks, while one in five happened in bike lanes or trails, according to a study conducted by The George Washington University Hospital. For the study over 100 e-scooter riders who ended up in their emergency room were interviewed between March and November 2019. E-scooter riders are far more likely to be injured or killed by cars than by each other. One of the key solutions to facilitating e-scooter safety is the availability of protected bike lanes.</span></p>
<p> </p>
