<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">E-scooter riders often navigate a gray area as they are sometimes forced onto sidewalks, where collisions with pedestrians occur. Other times, they share the road with cars, where they face a much greater risk. The lack of designated space increases their vulnerability, making infrastructure improvements a vital solution. Cities with extensive bike lane networks have seen up to 40% fewer e-scooter crashes compared to cities that lack bike lanes, according to the National Association of City Transportation Officials. Washington Area Bicyclist Association (WABA) believes a bike lane network can reduce e-scooter crashes by up to 35%. The need for protected bike lanes is especially urgent in areas with high e-scooter and bicycle ridership. Cities that invest in designated paths for micro mobility users have reported lower accident rates and improved the flow of traffic, according to the League of American Bicyclists. Bike lane networks that are well-integrated reduce confusion and provide a clear space for e-scooter users to ride freely, without the traffic of vehicles or pedestrians. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">A report from the DC Policy Center found that 62% of e-scooter riders feel unsafe due to poor infrastructure, including potholes and narrow streets. Many riders report avoiding certain routes altogether due to the high risk of collision. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">"Protected bike lanes correlate with lower injury rates among all micro mobility users. The safer the infrastructure, the fewer collisions," said Seth Grimes, the DC organizer WABA.</span></p>
<p> </p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Lime Micromobility, the largest electric vehicle company in the world, released a report last fall showing a decrease in scooter crashes directly corresponding with an increase in bike lane mileage and usage. The data shows that as cities expand infrastructure for micro mobility users, fewer e-scooter riders are injured in traffic accidents.</span></p>
<p> </p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">"If DC wants to truly commit to being a greener city, it should make e-scooters more accessible by improving infrastructure rather than restricting their use. Instead of policies that make it harder for residents to rely on scooters for short trips, the city should focus on expanding bike lanes and safer riding spaces to encourage sustainable transportation," said David Strickland, a former administrator of NHTSA.</span></p>
<p> </p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Despite the push for improved infrastructure, progress has been slow. City officials put focus on other transportation priorities. Micro mobility advocates argue that local politicians underestimate the urgency of bike lane expansion as e-scooters are still seen as a novelty, instead of a legitimate mode of transportation.</span></p>
<p> </p>
