</div>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">The Vice President for Research at the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety,  Jessica Cicchino, studied e-scooter crashes extensively, focusing on how they happen and how riders get injured. Her findings reveal that more e-scooter injuries occur on sidewalks than streets. Riders often flock to the sidewalk to avoid riding in unfazed traffic, mixed with motor vehicles. Ironically, riding on the sidewalk leads to unintended risks such as uneven pavement, pedestrians stepping into riders’ paths, and abrupt stops that force riders to swerve or fall. Cicchino explains that e-scooter riders are not choosing sidewalks because they want to, but because they feel they have to. Without protected bike lanes, riders are forced into dangerous situations, as they dodge pedestrians and uneven sidewalks.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div style="min-height: 346px;"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Beyond individual crashes, cities with strong bike lane networks consistently report lower injury rates for e-scooter users, proving that infrastructure plays a significant role in accident prevention. A report from the DC Policy Center found that 62% of e-scooter riders feel unsafe due to poor infrastructure, particularly the potholes and narrow streets.</span>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">The District Department of Transportation's Micromobility Dashboard tracks patterns in e-scooter usage, pointing to key locations where riders struggle to find safe passage. Areas lacking bike lanes tend to see higher reports of collisions or swerve to avoid crashing.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div id="datawrapper-vis-b5Bxp" style="min-height: 789px;">
<script type="text/javascript" defer="defer" src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/b5Bxp/embed.js" charset="utf-8" data-target="#datawrapper-vis-b5Bxp"></script>
<noscript><img src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/b5Bxp/full.png" alt="A chloropleth shows which wards have had the most crashes. The dek says 'Wards 5 and 8 have the most e-scooter crashes, which are labelled with having a high severity. Their bike lane presence is limited.'" /></noscript></div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">Businesses that rely on e-scooter services have also voiced frustration over the lack of infrastructure. Delivery couriers using e-scooters have reported challenges navigating streets safely, often forced to take detours or risk dangerous interactions with cars. They are expected to deliver items quickly, but they struggle to do so safely. </span></div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">“Navigating DC’s streets on an e-scooter for deliveries is unpredictable and often unsafe. I’m constantly forced to weave between traffic,” said Alex Ramirez, a delivery courier who relied on e-scooters for the past two years.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">Despite their risks, e-scooters have some safety advantages built into them. DC’s shared-mobility scooters are limited to speeds of 10 MPH, causing for less of an impact in case of a crash. Compared to cars or motorcycles, e-scooters also have less mass and momentum, making them less capable of causing severe damage in a crash. </span></div>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">Still, the overwhelming carnage caused by motor vehicles have made e-scooters a dangerous form of transportation. While e-scooters are often considered the sole problem for safety concerns, the data shows that drivers are overwhelmingly responsible for micro mobility deaths. The solution is not to restrict scooters, rather improving streets to make them safer for all forms of transportation.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">City officials have implemented some regulations to address micro mobility concerns, such as designated parking areas and speed restrictions. These measures still do not tackle the root issue, which is the need for more protected bike lanes and more policy to ensure the safety of riders.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div> </div>
<div><img class="post__image" src="file:///Users/nicoleclement/Documents/Publii/sites/smpa-3230/input/media/posts/6/bigger-e-scooter-timeline.png" alt="Timeline of e-scooter policies from when they popularized in DC to now." width="1798" height="1312"></div>
<p> </p>
<div> </div>
<div class="align-center"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Without substantial infrastructure improvements, riders will continue to face unnecessary risks. As scooter usage increases, DC has an opportunity to rethink urban mobility so teenagers like Tillman can safely ride e-scooters.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">E-scooters are just the beginning of a broader conversation about urban mobility and infrastructure, serving as a test for how cities are able to adapt to emerging transportation needs. While their convenience and accessibility make them a valuable tool for short trips, the lack of dedicated infrastructure forces riders into unsafe conditions. Without designated lanes, e-scooter users must constantly navigate a balancing act between crowded sidewalks and dangerous roads.</span></div>
<div> </div>
<div><span style="font-weight: 400;">More bike lanes would not only protect e-scooter users but also benefit bicyclists, pedestrians, and even drivers by reducing unpredictable distractions. As micro mobility continues to grow, the demand for better infrastructure will only intensify. Cities that prioritize safe travel spaces will see fewer crashes, along with minimal collisions between different transportation modes. With the right investments, urban areas can shift toward sustainable mobility that protects all road users.</span></div>
